  "answer": "Based on the SAMHSA report comparing 2021-2022 BRFSS state design-based estimates, the states with **past-month alcohol use prevalence below 45%** (BRFSS estimate) were:\n\n**States below 45% (2021-2022 BRFSS):**\n| State | BRFSS Estimate (2021-2022) | 95% CI |\n|-------|---------------------------|--------|\n| Utah | 33.19% | (32.31 – 34.06) |\n| West Virginia | 38.15% | (37.03 – 39.26) |\n| Kentucky | 37.50% | (36.12 – 38.89) |\n| Mississippi | 41.10% | (39.68 – 42.52) |\n| Alabama | 41.93% | (40.49 – 43.36) |\n| Oklahoma | 42.48% | (41.28 – 43.68) |\n| Arkansas | 43.66% | (42.23 – 45.08) |\n\n**Note on the timeframe:** This table shows **2021-2022 annual average BRFSS estimates** (pooled 2-year data). The BRFSS question used was: *\"During the past 30 days, how many days per week or per month did you have at least one drink of any alcoholic beverage?\"* — matching your definition exactly.\n\nFor single-year 2019 and 2020 estimates, you'd need to query the CDC BRFSS Prevalence & Trends Tool directly or access the annual BRFSS public-use datasets for those years, as this SAMHSA comparison report only publishes the 2021-2022 pooled estimates.",
